WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.170
If we have an animation movie or a computer
game with quadrupeds, and we are yearning

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:15.000
for really high-quality, lifelike animations,
motion capture is often the go-to tool for

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.279
the job.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:21.590
Motion capture means that we put an actor,
in our case, a dog in the studio, we ask it

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.860
to perform sitting, trotting, pacing and jumping,
record its motion, and transfer it onto our

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:28.860
virtual character.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.060
There are two key challenges with this approach.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.449
One, we have to try to weave together all
these motions, because we cannot record all

00:00:37.449 --> 00:00:43.129
the possible transitions between sitting and
pacing, jumping and trotting, and so on.

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:46.969
We need some filler animations to make these
transitions work.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:50.360
This was addressed by this neural network-based
technique here.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.660
The other one is trying to reduce these unnatural
foot sliding motions.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.029
Both of these have been addressed by learning-based
algorithms in the previous works that you

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:00.289
see here.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:06.539
Later, bipeds were also taught to maneuver
through complex geometry, and sit in not one

00:01:06.539 --> 00:01:10.740
kind of chair, but any chair, regardless of
geometry.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.689
This already sounds like science fiction.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:18.580
So, are we done, or can these amazing techniques
be further improved?

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:23.320
Well, we are talking about research, so the
answer is, of course, yes!

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.340
Here, you see a technique that reacts to its
environment in a believable manner.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:35.829
It can accidentally step on the ball, stagger
a little bit, and then flounder on this slippery

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.330
surface, and it doesn’t fall!

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.729
And it can do much, much more.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:47.710
The goal is that we would be able to do all
this without explicitly programming all of

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.479
these behaviors by hand.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:52.890
But, unfortunately, there is a problem.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.610
If we write an agent that behaves according
to physics, it will be difficult to control

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:58.679
properly.

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:03.479
And this is where this new technique shines
- it gives us physically appealing motion,

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:08.759
and we can grab a controller and play with
the character like in a video game.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.340
The first step we need to perform is called
imitation learning.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.670
This means looking at real, reference movement
data and trying to reproduce it.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.870
This is going to be motion that looks great,
is very natural, however, we are nowhere near

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:28.000
done, because we still don’t have any control
over this agent.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.409
Can we improve this somehow?

00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:32.790
Well, let’s try something and see if it
works!

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:38.640
This paper proposes that in step number two,
we try an architecture by the name generative

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.349
adversarial network.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:45.239
Here, we have a neural network that generates
motion, and a discriminator that looks at

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:49.489
these motions and tries to tell what is real,
and what is fake.

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:55.360
However, to accomplish this, we need lots
of real and fake data, that we then use to

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.599
train the discriminator to be able to tell
which one is which.

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:01.310
So how do we do that?

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:07.560
Well, let’s try to label the movement that
came from the user controller inputs as fake,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.950
and the reference movement data from before
as real.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.220
Remember that this makes sense as we concluded
that the reference motion looked natural.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:21.599
If we do this, over time, we will have a discriminator
neural network that is able to look at a piece

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:25.840
of animation data and tell whether it is real
or fake.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.200
So, after doing all this work, how does this
perform?

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.360
Does this work?

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:37.270
Well, sort of… but it does not react well
if we try to control the simulation.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:47.129
If we let it run undisturbed, it works beautifully,
and now, when we try to stop it with the controller…well,

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:49.200
this needs some more work, doesn’t it?

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.349
So, how do we adapt this architecture to the
animation problem we have here?

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:57.810
And here comes one of the key ideas of the
paper.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:04.579
In step number three, we can rewire this whole
thing to originate from the controller, and

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:08.680
introduce a deep reinforcement learning-based
fine tuning stage.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:13.750
This was the amazing technique that DeepMind
used to defeat Atari Breakout.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.290
So what good does all this for us?

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:22.350
Well, hold on to your papers, because it enables
true user control, while synthesizing motion

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.570
that is very robust against tough, previously
unseen scenarios.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:30.420
And if you have been watching this series
for a while, you know what is coming…of

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:36.100
course, throwing blocks at it and see how
well it can take the punishment.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.930
As you see, the AI is taking it like a champ.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:47.450
We can also add pathfinding to the agent,
and, of course, being computer graphics researchers,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.170
throw some blocks into the mix for good measure.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.460
It performs beautifully.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.590
This is so realistic.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.530
We can also add sensors to the agent to allow
them to navigate in this virtual world in

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.430
a realistic manner.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.480
Just a note on how remarkable this is.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.090
So this quadruped behaves according to physics,
lets us control it with a controller, which

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.920
is already somewhat of a contradiction.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.430
And, it is robust against these perturbations
at the same time.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:22.590
This is absolute witchcraft, and no doubt,
it has earned to be accepted to SIGGRAPH,

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:27.640
which is perhaps the most prestigious research
venue in computer graphics.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.400
Congratulations.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:47.220
What a

00:05:47.220 --> 00:06:07.960
time

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:21.870
to be alive!

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.930
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

